.Dd July 6, 2026
.Dt XTC_BDEV 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_bdev_open ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_close ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_logical_sector ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_physical_sector ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_capacity ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_pread ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_pwrite ,
.Nm xtc_bdev_flush
.Nd portable block-device I/O over async fibers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_bdev.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_bdev_open "const char *path" "int flags" "xtc_bdev_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_bdev_close "xtc_bdev_t *b"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn xtc_bdev_logical_sector "const xtc_bdev_t *b"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn xtc_bdev_physical_sector "const xtc_bdev_t *b"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_bdev_capacity "const xtc_bdev_t *b"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn xtc_bdev_pread "xtc_bdev_t *b" "void *buf" "size_t n" "uint64_t off"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn xtc_bdev_pwrite "xtc_bdev_t *b" "const void *buf" "size_t n" "uint64_t off"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_bdev_flush "xtc_bdev_t *b"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These provide a small, uniform surface for reading and writing a raw
block device -- or a regular file treated as one -- with
sector-aligned positioned I/O that suspends the calling fiber and
resumes it on completion, keeping the fiber's loop live for other work
while the I/O is outstanding.  They are built directly on
.Xr xtc_aio 3
and inherit its
.Em write once, runs as AIO everywhere
model: the read and write hot path routes through
.Fn xtc_aio_pread
and
.Fn xtc_aio_pwrite ,
and the flush through
.Fn xtc_aio_fsync .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_bdev_open
opens
.Fa path
and probes its geometry, storing the handle in
.Fa out .
.Fa flags
is a mask of
.Dv XTC_BDEV_READ
and
.Dv XTC_BDEV_WRITE .
The probe uses the host's native query:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
Linux:
.Dv BLKSSZGET
(logical sector),
.Dv BLKPBSZGET
(physical sector),
.Dv BLKGETSIZE64
(capacity); a real device is opened
.Dv O_DIRECT
so transfers bypass the page cache.
.It
FreeBSD and macOS:
.Dv DIOCGSECTORSIZE
and
.Dv DIOCGMEDIASIZE
.Pq the Darwin block-size / block-count IOCTLs on macOS .
.It
illumos and Solaris:
.Dv DKIOCGMEDIAINFO .
.It
Windows:
.Dv IOCTL_DISK_GET_DRIVE_GEOMETRY_EX
.Pq presently a defaults-returning stub .
.It
Fallback -- a regular file or an unqueryable target: logical 512,
physical 4096, capacity from
.Xr fstat 2 ,
buffered I/O.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xtc_bdev_close
closes the device and frees the handle;
.Dv NULL
is a no-op.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_bdev_logical_sector ,
.Fn xtc_bdev_physical_sector ,
and
.Fn xtc_bdev_capacity
report the probed logical sector size, physical sector size, and total
capacity in bytes.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_bdev_pread
reads
.Fa n
bytes into
.Fa buf ,
and
.Fn xtc_bdev_pwrite
writes
.Fa n
bytes from
.Fa buf ,
at absolute byte offset
.Fa off .
Both
.Fa off
and
.Fa n
must be multiples of the logical sector size; a misaligned request
returns
.Er XTC_E_INVAL
before any I/O is issued.
.Fn xtc_bdev_flush
flushes the device's data and metadata durably.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_bdev_pread
and
.Fn xtc_bdev_pwrite
return the number of bytes transferred
.Pq Ar >= 0 ;
a short read at end of device returns the partial count.  On failure
they return a negative
.Dv XTC_E_
code.
.Fn xtc_bdev_open
and
.Fn xtc_bdev_flush
return
.Dv XTC_OK
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_
code.
The size accessors return 0 on a
.Dv NULL
handle.
.Sh CONTEXT
Call
.Fn xtc_bdev_pread ,
.Fn xtc_bdev_pwrite ,
and
.Fn xtc_bdev_flush
from a fiber running on a loop for the async path; off a loop they run
synchronously on the calling thread, as with
.Xr xtc_aio 3 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Open a device, write and read back one logical sector:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_bdev_t *b;
if (xtc_bdev_open("/dev/sdb", XTC_BDEV_READ | XTC_BDEV_WRITE, &b) != XTC_OK)
	return -1;
uint32_t s = xtc_bdev_logical_sector(b);
if (xtc_bdev_pwrite(b, buf, s, 0) != (ssize_t)s) return -1;
if (xtc_bdev_flush(b) != XTC_OK) return -1;
if (xtc_bdev_pread(b, buf, s, 0) != (ssize_t)s) return -1;
xtc_bdev_close(b);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_aio 3 ,
.Xr xtc_fs 3 ,
.Xr xtc_loop 3
